The Gulf of Louyang is a {{w|gulf}} of the [[Shendan Ocean]] in western [[Lurandia]], lying between the western and south-western coasts of [[Sinchew]] and the northern coasts of [[Louyang]]. It has a surface area of approximately 1,379,492 km².

The gulf takes its name from [[Louyang]], the country whose landmass forms its western and southern shores. It is bounded by Sinchew to the north and north-east, and by Louyang to the west and south-west, and opens into the Shendan Ocean.

== Geography ==

The Gulf of Louyang occupies the corner of the Shendan Ocean where the landmass of Louyang approaches Sinchew from the west and south-west. Louyang's coast narrows the water at the south-western end, giving the gulf a partly enclosed character and sheltered waters by comparison with the open ocean beyond.

The gulf forms the whole of Sinchew's western and south-western coastline. The coast facing the gulf is Sinchew's principal sheltered shore, standing in contrast to the open [[Shendan Ocean]] coast along the country's northern margin, which faces deep water and is fully exposed to oceanic conditions. A broad shallow shelf extends seaward from Sinchew's western lowlands before the floor drops away into the central basin.

== Geology ==

The Gulf of Louyang occupies a rift basin, formed during the separation of the [[Sinchew Subplate]] from the landmass of Louyang. The gulf represents the rift arm that succeeded in opening to full oceanic width; the east–west rift valley of northern Sinchew is understood as a failed arm of the same system, an extensional branch that opened eastward into the subplate but never completed, leaving a trough within the continental crust rather than a new ocean basin.

The gulf floor is consequently rifted crust rather than a flooded continental platform, and the basin is correspondingly deep. Its maximum depth of approximately 4,300 m places it among the deeper marginal seas of its size, comparable in scale and depth to the Gulf of Mexico. The narrowing of the gulf at its south-western end, where Louyang's coast approaches Sinchew, may form a sill restricting exchange with the open Shendan Ocean beyond; if so, circulation in the deepest parts of the basin would be limited.

The gulf is a part of the [[Shendan Ocean]], which lies to the north and north-west of Sinchew and into which the gulf opens. It is distinct from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]], which Sinchew reaches only along a short stretch of coast at its north-eastern corner and which has no connection to the gulf.

== Hydrology ==

The gulf's hydrology is governed by three factors: its restricted connection to the open ocean, very large freshwater input from the wet coast of Sinchew, and the tidal regime of Adonia.

Circulation in the gulf is constrained by the narrowing at its south-western end, where the approach of Louyang's coast restricts exchange with the open [[Shendan Ocean]]. If that narrowing forms a sill, the deepest parts of the basin would be poorly ventilated, and deep water renewal would depend on intermittent overflow across the sill rather than on free exchange. On Adonia, the quadrennial Dragon Tide, which raises sea levels by 3 to 5 metres, provides the principal mechanism by which such a sill may be overtopped, making deep water renewal a periodic rather than continuous process.

Freshwater input is substantial. Sinchew's western seaboard is one of the wettest coasts on Adonia, and several large rivers draining the interior plateau and the eastern highlands reach the gulf. Approximate annual discharge into the gulf is on the order of several hundred cubic kilometres. This input freshens the surface waters along the Sinchew coast and produces strong vertical stratification, with a comparatively fresh surface layer overlying denser water below.

Tides are mixed and driven by Adonia's two moons, [[Imera]] and [[Theia]], which together raise sea levels by roughly one metre under ordinary conditions. The two bodies produce complex interference patterns, so that tidal range and timing vary considerably along the coast. Every four years, on the intercalary day of [[February 31st]], the gas giant [[Quetzal]] passes between Adonia and the star Aurelius, generating the Dragon Tide — a surge of 3 to 5 metres that affects the gulf as it does the rest of the planet's coasts.

The gulf shares in the characteristic turquoise colouration of Adonian oceans, which derives from mineral-rich plume water released from submarine hydrothermal vents and dispersed by ocean currents.

